The tricky part here is that protobuf shape in JSON format is different from the one you get when you look at protobuf object in TS. 

Given:
```
message Abc {
   oneof foo {
      string bar = 1;
      string qux = 2;
   } 
}
```
the JSON representation of this object is, for example
```json
{
   "bar": "some value"
}
```
or
```json
{
   "qux": "another value"
}
```
as you can see, `oneof foo` is not represented, and that makes sense because it is not a real field (it is not even assigned with an ID).

These JSONs above are parseable by both java and TS protobuf parsers, because they can actually figure out what `oneOfKind` value is there. But if you just `JSON.stringify` proto in TS, you'll get:
```json
{
   "foo": {
      "oneOfKind": "bar",
      "bar": "some value"
   }
}
```
which makes sense because it is basically a dump of the object's internal state, it is NOT the proper protobuf JSON serialization. Similar thing may happen in java if you just `someProto.toString()`, that's not how you serialize protobuf JSONs, it's just a way to dump internal object's state.

